# CAT-2D.2: the Family A evidence artifact is checksummed in
# docs/cat-2d2-evidence/manifest.json, and that checksum is stamped into every
# alias row's `evidence` payload by
# docs/sql/cat-2d2-1-family-a-reconciliation.sql.
#
# Without this rule, core.autocrlf=true rewrites the CSV's line endings on
# checkout and the working-tree bytes stop matching the approved artifact.
# Pinning it to LF keeps the committed evidence byte-stable on every platform.
docs/cat-2d2-evidence/*.csv text eol=lf

# ATTR-0: same reasoning. docs/attr-0-evidence/manifest.json carries a SHA-256
# per CSV, and scripts/attr0-variant-attribution-probe.mjs --check re-derives
# those hashes to prove the committed artifacts still match upstream. The probe
# normalises newlines before hashing, so --check survives CRLF either way — but
# pinning LF keeps the working-tree bytes identical to the committed bytes, so a
# reviewer hashing the file by hand gets the manifest value.
docs/attr-0-evidence/*.csv text eol=lf

# F-15: the durable attribution correction migration is checksummed in
# docs/F-15_IMPLEMENTATION.md and in the PR that proposes it for execution.
# The whole point of that checksum is that a reviewer can hash the file by hand
# and get the same value before authorising a production run, so the
# working-tree bytes must equal the committed bytes on every platform.
docs/sql/f15-durable-attribution-correction*.sql text eol=lf
